--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -85,6 +85,20 @@
 Configuration/entry observations are **as read**, bound to their captured input identities and hashes. They are not a locked filesystem snapshot; consumers must revalidate them at their own transaction boundary. `expected.new_entry_default_name` is the default for creating an entry; an existing matching entry retains its observed display name. Raw lines are not a complete interpretation of Limine URIs. Hash-tool availability depends on the reported process PATH and needs a fresh check when using that policy. No producer-issued filesystem initialization, logging or mutation is performed; normal filesystem access-time behavior is not suppressed.
 
 The interface describes an explicit native operation. Package-declared kernel inventory, shell build-mode/fallback selection and actual producer completion remain with their respective owners.
+
+### Shell build-settings description
+
+```sh
+limine-mkinitcpio --describe-build linux 6.19.1-arch1-1 linux-lts 6.12.1-1-lts
+```
+
+The first-position `--describe-build` route takes explicit package/version pairs, including declarations whose files are missing. It preserves their order and duplicates. It does not discover installed kernels, select a transaction's work or authenticate. One JSON document has `format: limine-build-description`, `schema: 1`, `scope: shell-settings`, typed `complete`/`errors`, as-read configuration digests, raw/effective settings with their origin, observed UEFI mode, cleaned kernel names, fallback choices and exact additional UKI argument tokens. Exit 0 means a complete settings observation, 1 incomplete observation, and 2 invalid operands. Optional absent configuration is described; unreadable files/directories, invalid UTF-8 and unsupported shell-control assignments make the result incomplete.
+
+The query shares the shell's four-layer traversal, line parser, ASCII-space name cleaning, exact `yes`/fallback predicates and `read -ra` option splitting with production. Inherited values are retained unless assigned. Quoting is not shell evaluation: the existing parser strips a boundary double quote, preserves trailing spaces and ignores indexed/`+=` assignments; quotes within `MKINITCPIO_UKI_OPTIONS` remain literal tokens, and `read` consumes its first line. Configured IFS participates in that split. The query records other shell-control effects as incomplete instead of applying configuration as its execution environment; mutation retains its existing exports and dynamically scoped loader behavior.
+
+No machine ID, ESP, command-line file or state is initialized. No authentication, terminal probe, lock, hook, logger, sbctl/Snapper query, native publication or builder runs. Inputs are as-read observations, not an atomic filesystem snapshot. Consumers must repeat their observation before relying on it.
+
+Additional UKI arguments follow the generated builder arguments and may override them; their effects on mkinitcpio are explicitly unresolved. UKI command-line omission is conditional on the current `is_sb_installed` and `is_snapper_installed` helper predicates. Those predicates inspect output text without validating the producing commands' results; this query neither runs them nor upgrades that contract. Regular builds use `--no-cmdline` and do not consume the additional UKI arguments. Native destination rules, source-basename/operation mapping, actual publication, signing and boot proof remain separate producer/consumer responsibilities.
 </details>
 
